\description{
A call to \code{gp()} can be used in the \code{formula} argument of
model construction functions in the \code{epinowcast} package such as
\code{\link[=enw_formula]{enw_formula()}}. It declares a Hilbert-space reduced-rank
(spectral) approximate Gaussian process indexed by \code{time} (and
optionally a grouping variable \code{by}) whose value at each observation
is added to the linear predictor. As with \code{\link[=arima]{arima()}}, arguments are
not evaluated; they are passed by name for use in model construction.

Like \code{\link[=arima]{arima()}} and \code{\link[=rw]{rw()}}, a \code{gp()} term works on every module that
takes a formula, each with its own prior prefix:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=enw_expectation]{enw_expectation()}} — the growth rate (\code{expr}) and the latent-to-obs
proportion (\code{expl}).
\item \code{\link[=enw_reference]{enw_reference()}} — the parametric delay mean (\code{refp}) and the
non-parametric logit hazards (\code{refnp}).
\item \code{\link[=enw_report]{enw_report()}} — report-date logit hazards (\code{rep}).
\item \code{\link[=enw_missing]{enw_missing()}} — the missing-reference proportion (\code{miss}).
}

At most one \code{gp()} term is currently supported per formula (the
multiple-term example shown for \code{\link[=gp_terms]{gp_terms()}} only illustrates term
detection, not a supported model). The default \code{alpha} (magnitude)
and length-scale priors are inherited from \code{EpiNow2} and are set on
\code{EpiNow2}'s scale; on a given module's scale (for example the log
growth rate or a logit hazard) they may need tuning with
\code{\link[=enw_replace_priors]{enw_replace_priors()}}.
}
\details{
When \code{basis_prop} and/or \code{boundary_scale} are \code{NULL}, they are
chosen from the 5\%/95\% quantiles of the \code{ls_meanlog}/\code{ls_sdlog}
length-scale prior using the relationships of Riutort-Mayol et al.
(2023, Section 4.3.1): the boundary factor from the upper quantile
(longer length scales need a wider boundary) and the number of basis
functions from the lower quantile (shorter length scales need more
basis functions), capped at one basis function per free time point.
This is a one-shot, prior-based approximation of that paper's own
iterative, posterior-based procedure, and depends on \code{time}'s
absolute scale: a reference length scale that is short relative to
the span of \code{time} chooses a large, possibly capped, basis. This is
opt-in, not the default (see \code{basis_prop}), because tested against
this package's typical \code{gp()} usage (few distinct \code{time} values
against the default reference prior) it produced far more basis
functions than time points and degraded sampling. This package
does not (yet) warn post-fit when the posterior length scale falls
outside the range the chosen settings represent accurately.
